{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Seven days you shall eat unleavened bread.</b> Rashi's explanation: elsewhere it says 'six days you shall eat unleavened bread' etc. — this teaches about the seventh day that there is no obligation to eat matzah, provided only that one does not eat leaven. And from where do we know that all six days are optional? This is a rule of the Torah: something that was in the general category etc. That is: the seven days were in the general category of eating matzah, as it is written 'seven days you shall eat unleavened bread,' and the seventh went out of the category of seven from this verse 'six days you shall eat unleavened bread,' to teach about it that it is optional, not obligatory. And it does not go out to teach about itself alone — rather, it goes out to teach about the entire general category, namely the seven days: what the seventh is optional — as it is written 'and on the seventh day a solemn assembly,' and it is not written 'you shall eat unleavened bread,' since it removed it from the category of the others — even the six days too are optional: if one wanted to eat without bread or to fast, the option is in his hand. One might say even the first night is optional — it says 'in the evening you shall eat unleavened bread,' the verse established it as obligatory. 'Seven days you shall eat unleavened bread' — corresponding to a quarter of the month, which is seven days, for thus the plague of darkness operated. 'Seven days you shall eat unleavened bread' — this commandment is for all generations. And it is attached to 'an everlasting statute you shall celebrate it.' And one cannot say this refers to the matzot they took out of Egypt, for even though they had enough from them for sixty-one meals, the commandment was only for the first night." "<b>But on the first day you shall remove.</b> According to the plain sense, he explained by analogy with 'and Elohim finished on the seventh day' — for one cannot say that He performed labor on the seventh day, but rather He finished all His labor on the sixth day, and when the seventh day came, all His labor was found to be completed. So too here: <b>on the first day you shall remove</b> — before the first day arrives you shall have removed, and when the first day comes, it is found to be already removed." "<b>For whoever eats leaven.</b> Lest you say one may eat matzah and eat leaven with it — if so, the matzah would be nullified on account of the leaven, for a person is accustomed to it. 'For whoever eats leaven' — this is the punishment; from where have we heard the warning? It says 'and shall not eat leaven.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The phrase 'דבר שהיה בכלל וכו׳' alludes to the hermeneutical rule 'כלל ופרט' continued with 'ופרט' and 'יצא ללמד על הכלל כולו יצא'; resolved 'וכו׳' mechanically as 'etc.' without expanding."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:18.676330044Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}